  "foaf:name": "Assessing spatiotemporal dynamics in herring population structure under climate change (HERPOPS)",
  "sdo:name": "Assessing spatiotemporal dynamics in herring population structure under climate change (HERPOPS)",
  "sdo:description": "Understanding the spatiotemporal occurrence of marine populations is crucial for management. Traditional methods often failed to resolve population structure, but genomic tools now provide clarity. The Atlantic herring (Clupea harengus), a key species in the north-eastern Atlantic, has a complex structure influenced by climate change. HERPOPS uses innovative genomic research, including century-long data, to explore these processes. By applying diagnostic SNP markers, it identifies the population origin of individual herring across space and time. Environmental DNA (eDNA) will reveal small-scale dynamics of spring and autumn spawners and potential spawning-type switching. HERPOPS integrates genomic results into new assessment models and management strategies to ensure sustainable exploitation and maintain biodiversity, directly informing ecosystem-based fisheries advice.",
  "sdo:location": "Norway",
  "sdo:identifier": "NOR10",
  "sdo:url": "https://oceandecade.org/actions/assessing-spatiotemporal-dynamics-in-herring-population-structure-herpops/",
  "sdo:license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",
  "sdo:parentOrganization": {
    "@type": "sdo:Organization",
    "sdo:name": "Institute of Marine Research"
  },
